{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "05PY", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\text{id}_X \\in \\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(X, X)$", "MATH_0002": "$X \\to M_i$", "MATH_0003": "$i$", "MATH_0004": "$X \\to M_i \\to X$", "MATH_0005": "$$\n\\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, X)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\colim_i \\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, M_i)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, X)\n$$", "MATH_0006": "$W$", "MATH_0007": "$X \\to M_i$", "MATH_0008": "$$\n\\colim_i \\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, M_i)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, X)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\colim_i \\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, M_i)\n$$", "MATH_0009": "$W = M_j$", "MATH_0010": "$\\text{id}_{M_j}$", "MATH_0011": "$M_j \\to X \\to M_i \\to M_k$", "MATH_0012": "$M_j \\to M_k$", "MATH_0013": "$k$", "MATH_0014": "$k$", "MATH_0015": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0016": "$W = M_k$", "MATH_0017": "$M_k \\to X$", "MATH_0018": "$j$", "MATH_0019": "$k \\to j$", "MATH_0020": "$i \\to j$", "MATH_0021": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0022": "$M_k \\to X \\to M_i \\to M_j$", "MATH_0023": "$M_k \\to M_j$", "MATH_0024": "$(X, \\{M_k \\to X\\})$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\n\\end{proof}\n\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_text": "It is clear that (3) implies (2). Assume (2).\nThen <MATH_0001> comes from a morphism <MATH_0002>\nfor some <MATH_0003>, i.e., <MATH_0004> is the identity. Then both maps\n<MATH_0005>\nare bijective for all <MATH_0006> where the first one is induced by the morphism\n<MATH_0007> we found above, and the composition is the identity. This means\nthat the composition\n<MATH_0008>\nis the identity too. Setting <MATH_0009> and starting with <MATH_0010>\nin the colimit, we see that <MATH_0011> is equal to\n<MATH_0012> for some <MATH_0013> large enough. This proves (1) holds.\n\nAssume (4). Let <MATH_0014> be an object of <MATH_0015>. Setting <MATH_0016>\nwe deduce there exists a unique morphism <MATH_0017> such that\nthere exists a <MATH_0018> and morphisms <MATH_0019> and <MATH_0020> in <MATH_0021>\nsuch that <MATH_0022> is equal to <MATH_0023>.\nThe uniqueness guarantees that we obtain a cocone <MATH_0024>.\nIn this way we see that (4) implies (2); some details omitted.\n\nWe omit the proof that (1) implies the other conditions.", "unit_id": "tag:05PY:proof", "source_text_hash": "sha256:84c7bdee00330a2d60c01dd652ffe131f65bb6b564fec27947f46c49fd0fbf2a", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1bc4dcedbe6098b14b56fc16c4e906d444ee75ba6098814125c75f3b45a064cd", "source_math_hash": "sha256:305758c706f92600848880d48f527bcf97e26b630fde4620c6bec03a8f6ccf53"}
